{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he went out, etc., to meet his father-in-law.</b> Meaning: since Jethro said to him, 'If you do not come out for me, etc.,' therefore the verse says <b>and Moses went out to meet his father-in-law</b> — for the honor of his father-in-law was sufficient for him to go out to meet him. Or perhaps it made the matter known that he went out specifically to meet him, and did not turn in his going out toward the tent of the woman and her children, or that he would state explicitly that his going out was to meet his father-in-law. Also according to their words, of blessed memory (Mekhilta), that it was Moses who prostrated himself to Jethro — this is your proof that it was for his honor that he went out; and therefore they said, of blessed memory (ibid.): 'Great honor was paid to Jethro,' etc. And apart from our reasoning, whence would he [derive] honor from this going out? Perhaps Moses went out for the honor of his children and his wife?" "<b>And he prostrated himself, etc.</b> Our Sages, of blessed memory (Mekhilta Yitro 1), said: I do not know who prostrated himself to whom. When it says 'man' (אישׁ) — who is the one called 'man' (אישׁ)? This is Moses. End quote. Behold, the reason they did not say that both of them prostrated themselves one to the other is because it uses a singular expression: 'and he prostrated himself and kissed him,' which indicates that only one prostrated himself. And if you would say, according to their words of blessed memory — but do we not find that Jethro too is called 'man' (אישׁ), as it is written (Exodus 2:21) ״וַיּוֹאֶל מֹשֶׁה לָשֶׁבֶת אֶת הָאִישׁ״ — one must distinguish between the appellation 'man' (אישׁ) applied to Jethro and the appellation 'man' (אישׁ) applied to Moses. But first I will raise [the question] at dawn: what distinction is there in this name 'man' (אישׁ)? After all, every person is called 'man' (אישׁ) and his female counterpart 'woman' (אשָּׁה), and even among animals we find that the verse says in the portion of Noah (Genesis 7:2) ״שִׁבְעָה אִישׁ וְאִשְׁתּוֹ״. Rather, the name 'man' (אישׁ) changes [in meaning] when it appears in place of the person's name and the appellation 'man' (אישׁ) is added to him — this indicates a term of distinction. And in a place where it says 'man' (אישׁ) without mention of his name, this occurs in place of the name and does not indicate distinction, as you may say (Genesis 24:21) ״וְהָאִישׁ מִשְׁתָּאֵה לָהּ״, ״וַיִּמְצָאֵהוּ אִישׁ״ (Genesis 37:15). Also the mention of 'men' (אֲנָשִׁים) of which they said, of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 16), that it is a term of importance — only when accompanied by mention of their name, as it is written (Numbers 13:3) ״כֻּלָּם אֲנָשִׁים״ — for after mentioning their names he returned to say 'all of them men' (״כֻּלָּם אֲנָשִׁים״), this indicates a term of importance. But when this appellation appears in place of the mention of their name, it is not a distinction, as it says (Numbers 22:9) ״מִי הָאֲנָשִׁים הָאֵלֶּה עִמָּךְ״. Now behold, we find that the verse says (Exodus 11:3) ״גַּם הָאִישׁ מֹשֶׁה״, ״כִּי זֶה מֹשֶׁה הָאִישׁ״ (Exodus 32:23) — here he mentioned his name and added to say 'the man' (הָאִישׁ); this indicates an expression of greatness and lordship. And this is what they said: Who is the one called 'man' (אישׁ)? This is Moses — meaning 'man' (אישׁ) as a term of rulership and dominion. But Jethro, although we find him called 'man' (אישׁ), we do not find it with the addition except in place of the mention of his name he said 'the man' (הָאִישׁ), and that is a name that applies to every male creature." "And from this point on, when the verse says here <b>man</b> (אישׁ), the matter is balanced: whether it is the ordinary appellation denoting maleness, or its meaning is 'the man' (אישׁ) in the sense noted above of importance. And this word rests equally between Jethro and Moses — if we say its meaning is an appellation for a male, it enters the sense for Jethro as well, and both are balanced in the meaning; yet I cannot make it refer to both of them, since the verse said only one, as we deduced from its saying 'and he prostrated himself' (וַיִּשְׁתַּחוּ) [singular]. And this one — I do not know who he is; and the verse did not come to obscure but to explain. And necessarily one must say that the meaning of <b>man</b> (אישׁ) stated here is a term of importance, and in saying so he is compelled to say that this is Moses, the 'man' (אישׁ) to whom this importance is uniquely ascribed and not to Jethro — and in this the verse did not obscure its intent, for it has explained that it is about Moses that it speaks."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:29:47.289895872Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}